\title{fivenumMeta: Five-Number Summaries in Meta-Analysis, Imputed Means and SDs with Standard Errors}
\description{
Meta-analyses often pool studies that report a median with quartiles or
extremes rather than a mean and a standard deviation, and the established
estimators impute the mean and the SD from those summaries. An imputed mean
is not a sample mean, and its standard deviation is not
\eqn{\sigma/\sqrt{n}}. This package computes, under normality, the standard
deviation factors \eqn{C_\mu(n)} and \eqn{C_\sigma(n)} such that
\eqn{SD(\hat\mu) = C_\mu(n)\,\sigma} and \eqn{SD(\hat\sigma) = C_\sigma(n)\,\sigma},
and the inflation factors \eqn{\kappa_\mu(n) = C_\mu(n)\sqrt{n}} and
\eqn{\kappa_\sigma(n) = C_\sigma(n)/\sqrt{1 - b_S(n)^2}}, exactly, from the
means and covariances of the normal order statistics that the package
\pkg{osmoments} computes, at every sample size. The estimators covered are the
best linear unbiased estimator (BLUE) of the reported values, the default, and
the estimators of Wan, Luo and Shi, under the data-reporting scenarios
\code{"S1"} (minimum, median, maximum), \code{"S2"} (the three quartiles),
\code{"S3"} (all five), and, for the BLUE, \code{"S4"} and \code{"S5"}, which
add the 10th and 90th percentiles to \code{"S2"} and \code{"S3"}.
}
\details{
For a meta-analysis data set, one row per study, \code{\link{se_imputed_df}}
reads the scenario off the columns each row has and appends the imputed mean
and SD with their naive and exact plug-in standard errors, and
\code{\link{se_imputed_diff}} does the same for two groups per row and adds
the effect of the study, the difference of means or the log variability ratio,
with its naive and its exact standard error, in the names that
\code{metafor::rma()} takes as they are. \code{\link{pool_imputed}} pools
those studies in one call, under the naive and the exact standard errors side
by side, by the common-effect or the DerSimonian-Laird model. For one study,
\code{\link{se_imputed}} returns the same quantities from the reported values
and the sample size. The factors themselves are \code{\link{c_mu}},
\code{\link{c_sigma}} and \code{\link{inflation_factors}}, with the
closed-form shortcut formulas \code{\link{c_mu_shortcut}} and
\code{\link{c_sigma_shortcut}} for use without the integration. The weights
and the reported ranks are \code{\link{estimator_weights}} and
\code{\link{scenario_ranks}}, and \code{\link{imputed_moments}} gives the
variances and the covariance of the two imputed statistics. Every function
defaults to the BLUE; the estimators of Wan, Luo and Shi are reached by
\code{version = "wan_luo_shi"} in the study-level functions and in
\code{inflation_factors()}, and by the argument \code{method} in the others.
}
